## 5. The execution pipeline

Every run follows the same fixed sequence:

```
 1  select Transaction + Environment + Data Profile
 2  Edge launches (Selenium WebDriver + CDP)
 3  target URL opens
 4  login with Teller ID + password              ← user-supplied
 5  forced Change Password window  →  TRANSACTION 9750, auto-attached
 6  automatic re-login with the new password
 7  search for the target transaction
 8  execute the recorded workflow with resolved test data
 9  logout
10  TELLER RESET — CBS reset string over a socket   ← ALWAYS, even after failure
11  detailed HTML report
```

Step 10 is a cleanup node in the LangGraph workflow *and* a `finally` block in the
Java runner. The teller is never left locked or dirty.

---

## 6. Test data rules

Banking data — CIF, account numbers, PAN, IFSC, branch codes, maker/checker IDs,
teller ID, passwords — is **always supplied by you**. The AI may generate only
cosmetic, non-validated filler (remarks / narration), and only when asked.

Resolution order at execution time:

```
1. existing framework data     java-framework/config/*.properties
2. profile / saved values      Profile Manager + Test Data Editor
3. runtime input               prompted on the Execute screen
4. database query              configured SQL per field
5. API data                    configured endpoint per field
6. AI-generated                cosmetic fields only
```

Feature files reference variables, never literals:

```gherkin
When user enters "<CustomerName>" in "Customer Name"
And user enters "<PAN>" in "PAN"
```

Changing a value tomorrow means editing
`java-framework/test-data/<ENV>/transaction_<id>.json` — the feature file, page
object, step definitions and runner stay untouched.

